1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,775
Sam was about to run Harlan's next batch the old way, out of habit: four variants,

2
00:00:04,775 --> 00:00:08,876
the same number a shoot would have given him, because four felt like plenty.

3
00:00:08,876 --> 00:00:12,761
Dana asked one question before he hit generate: is four actually enough?

4
00:00:12,761 --> 00:00:17,294
Neither of them had an answer that wasn't a guess, so that's what this lecture turns

5
00:00:17,294 --> 00:00:21,719
"generate more variants" into: an actual number, because as advice goes, "more" is

6
00:00:21,719 --> 00:00:22,151
useless.

7
00:00:22,151 --> 00:00:22,960
More than what?

8
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:23,824
Enough for what?

9
00:00:23,824 --> 00:00:27,547
Here's the honest answer, worked out properly rather than guessed at.

10
00:00:27,547 --> 00:00:31,918
If a real winner shows up roughly once in every twelve variants, the illustrative

11
00:00:31,918 --> 00:00:36,397
rate from Section zero, then generating only four of them gives you worse odds than

12
00:00:36,397 --> 00:00:38,232
a coin flip of catching it at all.

13
00:00:38,232 --> 00:00:42,765
That should bother you a little, because four variants is roughly what a traditional

14
00:00:42,765 --> 00:00:47,136
shoot gets you, and it's exactly the batch size most accounts were running before

15
00:00:47,136 --> 00:00:48,323
any of this got cheap.

16
00:00:48,323 --> 00:00:50,535
The old constraint wasn't just expensive.

17
00:00:50,535 --> 00:00:54,205
It was, without anyone realising it, statistically unlikely to work.

18
00:00:54,755 --> 00:00:58,237
If you want the mechanism behind this number, here it is, once.

19
00:00:58,237 --> 00:01:02,161
"P" is the hit rate: how often a variant turns out to be a real winner.

20
00:01:02,161 --> 00:01:06,637
Say it's about one in twelve, the illustrative rate from earlier in this section.

21
00:01:06,637 --> 00:01:08,738
"N" is how many variants you generate.

22
00:01:08,738 --> 00:01:13,049
The formula says your chance of catching at least one winner is one, minus the

23
00:01:13,049 --> 00:01:17,525
chance you miss every single time, and because each extra variant multiplies that

24
00:01:17,525 --> 00:01:22,113
miss chance down, a modest increase in batch size buys a surprisingly large jump in

25
00:01:22,113 --> 00:01:23,052
your actual odds.

26
00:01:23,052 --> 00:01:27,639
If you don't want the mechanism, skip straight to the number that actually matters,

27
00:01:27,639 --> 00:01:28,634
on the next slide.

28
00:01:28,634 --> 00:01:32,669
Twenty variants gets you to roughly four-to-one odds of finding a winner.

29
00:01:32,669 --> 00:01:37,146
That's the decision this whole formula is standing in for, and it's the only part

30
00:01:37,146 --> 00:01:38,583
you need to carry forward.

31
00:01:39,133 --> 00:01:43,243
Read this chart slowly, because every point is a real decision someone makes.

32
00:01:43,243 --> 00:01:46,819
At four variants, a single shoot's worth, you're below a coin flip.

33
00:01:46,819 --> 00:01:48,688
At eight, you're roughly even odds.

34
00:01:48,688 --> 00:01:50,983
At twelve, you're better than two in three.

35
00:01:50,983 --> 00:01:52,905
At twenty, you're past four in five.

36
00:01:52,905 --> 00:01:57,122
Notice what happens after that: forty variants, double the batch, only buys you

37
00:01:57,122 --> 00:01:58,349
another fifteen points.

38
00:01:58,349 --> 00:02:00,805
Most of the value is already banked by twenty.

39
00:02:00,805 --> 00:02:04,381
Read the gap between the first three points and the last two again.

40
00:02:04,381 --> 00:02:08,705
Going from four to twelve variants, a threefold increase, takes you from a losing

41
00:02:08,705 --> 00:02:10,306
bet to a comfortably good one.

42
00:02:10,306 --> 00:02:14,737
Going from twenty to forty, also a doubling, buys you almost nothing by comparison.

43
00:02:14,737 --> 00:02:19,061
The curve is steep early and flat late, and knowing where the flat part starts is

44
00:02:19,061 --> 00:02:21,303
the actual skill this lecture is teaching.

45
00:02:21,853 --> 00:02:26,302
This table actually prevents two separate mistakes, and they cost you in opposite

46
00:02:26,302 --> 00:02:26,906
directions.

47
00:02:26,906 --> 00:02:31,301
Too few variants, and you'll conclude a perfectly good product or angle "doesn't

48
00:02:31,301 --> 00:02:35,805
work on paid social", when the honest conclusion is that you never gave the test a

49
00:02:35,805 --> 00:02:37,508
fair chance to find its winner.

50
00:02:37,508 --> 00:02:42,122
Too many, chasing the last few points of certainty, and you're spending real testing

51
00:02:42,122 --> 00:02:46,737
budget for almost no extra chance of a better outcome, money that would do more good

52
00:02:46,737 --> 00:02:48,769
scaling the winner you already found.

53
00:02:48,769 --> 00:02:51,461
Both mistakes get blamed on the wrong thing, too.

54
00:02:51,461 --> 00:02:55,910
A too-small batch that finds nothing gets written up as "AI creative doesn't work

55
00:02:55,910 --> 00:03:00,195
for us." A too-large batch that finds a winner gets written up as thoroughness

56
00:03:00,195 --> 00:03:04,644
paying off, when really it just spent extra money to arrive at roughly the answer

57
00:03:04,644 --> 00:03:06,897
twenty variants would already have given.

58
00:03:07,447 --> 00:03:11,224
So here's the number worth carrying forward: somewhere around twenty.

59
00:03:11,224 --> 00:03:15,824
You'll see that exact batch size again more than once in this course, and it's not a

60
00:03:15,824 --> 00:03:16,481
coincidence.

61
00:03:16,481 --> 00:03:20,915
It's the smallest number that gets you comfortably past four-in-five odds without

62
00:03:20,915 --> 00:03:25,241
tipping into the flat part of the curve where extra variants stop earning their

63
00:03:25,241 --> 00:03:25,514
keep.

64
00:03:25,514 --> 00:03:27,595
It's not a universal constant, either.

65
00:03:27,595 --> 00:03:32,085
If your product sits in a more forgiving category, or the hit rate on your account

66
00:03:32,085 --> 00:03:36,574
genuinely runs higher than one in twelve, the same table gets you to a good number

67
00:03:36,574 --> 00:03:36,957
faster.

68
00:03:36,957 --> 00:03:41,502
What matters is the method, not the specific twenty: work out roughly how often you

69
00:03:41,502 --> 00:03:45,115
find a real winner, then read the table until the curve goes flat.

70
00:03:45,665 --> 00:03:48,925
One honest limit before you take this number and run with it.

71
00:03:48,925 --> 00:03:53,307
The formula assumes each variant is a genuinely separate shot at finding the tail.

72
00:03:53,307 --> 00:03:57,796
If your twenty variants are really four ideas with five colour swaps each, you don't

73
00:03:57,796 --> 00:04:02,177
have twenty shots, you have four, and the odds in that table collapse back down to

74
00:04:02,177 --> 00:04:02,979
the losing end.

75
00:04:02,979 --> 00:04:07,414
Volume only works if it's covering real ground, and covering real ground is exactly

76
00:04:07,414 --> 00:04:10,086
what the brief in Section two exists to guarantee.

77
00:04:10,086 --> 00:04:14,575
This is also the honest answer to "why not just generate two hundred variants and be

78
00:04:14,575 --> 00:04:15,323
done with it".

79
00:04:15,323 --> 00:04:19,812
Past a certain point, you're not adding new angles, you're adding near-duplicates of

80
00:04:19,812 --> 00:04:24,194
ones you already tried, and near-duplicates don't behave like independent shots no

81
00:04:24,194 --> 00:04:27,774
matter how the maths on the formula slide would like to treat them.

82
00:04:27,774 --> 00:04:31,995
Coverage, not raw count, is what the number twenty is actually standing in for.

83
00:04:32,545 --> 00:04:37,322
Next lecture is the last one before the exception: the one place a bigger production

84
00:04:37,322 --> 00:04:41,814
budget still beats this whole approach, and exactly where that line sits for an

85
00:04:41,814 --> 00:04:43,065
account like Harlan's.

86
00:04:43,065 --> 00:04:46,533
Before that, do the five-minute version for your own product.

87
00:04:46,533 --> 00:04:51,196
You don't need Harlan's exact hit rate, you need your own honest guess at it, read

88
00:04:51,196 --> 00:04:53,584
against the table on this lecture's slide.

89
00:04:53,584 --> 00:04:56,484
Write down the batch size that guess points you to.

90
00:04:56,484 --> 00:05:00,465
It's the number Section 2.6's brief, artifact B01, asks you for first.
